 questions: [
  ["Which of the following is the first event in primary haemostasis after a vessel is cut?","Platelets adhere to exposed collagen via von Willebrand factor",["Fibrin is deposited over the wound","Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin","Plasmin is activated"],"Adhesion through vWF and glycoprotein Ib comes first; fibrin and thrombin belong to secondary haemostasis, and plasmin to fibrinolysis."],
  ["A 70-year-old takes aspirin 81 mg daily after a stent. Tooth 1.6 is to be extracted. The most appropriate management is to:","Continue aspirin and use local haemostatic measures",["Stop aspirin 7 days before and resume after healing","Stop aspirin on the morning of surgery","Replace aspirin with warfarin for one week"],"Stopping antiplatelet therapy risks stent thrombosis, which far outweighs manageable oozing. The 7-day stop is tempting because it matches the platelet lifespan, but the risk calculus makes it wrong."],
  ["Which laboratory test is used to monitor warfarin therapy?","INR",["aPTT","Bleeding time","Platelet count"],"Warfarin lowers the vitamin K dependent factors, which the prothrombin time (reported as INR) measures. aPTT monitors unfractionated heparin."],
  ["A patient on warfarin for atrial fibrillation needs extraction of 4.6. An INR taken the previous day is 2.4. The most appropriate plan is to:","Proceed without altering warfarin, using local haemostatic measures",["Stop warfarin for three days then extract","Halve the warfarin dose for a week before","Refer for extraction under general anaesthesia"],"An INR within the usual therapeutic range permits simple extraction with local measures; interrupting warfarin exposes the patient to thromboembolism."],
  ["Warfarin produces its anticoagulant effect by:","Inhibiting the vitamin K dependent synthesis of factors II, VII, IX and X",["Directly inhibiting thrombin","Blocking platelet thromboxane synthesis","Activating antithrombin III"],"Warfarin blocks vitamin K recycling so the liver cannot carboxylate these factors. Heparin acts through antithrombin; dabigatran inhibits thrombin directly."],
  ["A 9-year-old boy with known haemophilia A requires extraction of 7.5. Which test result is expected?","Prolonged aPTT with normal PT and platelet count",["Prolonged PT with normal aPTT","Low platelet count with normal aPTT","Prolonged bleeding time with normal aPTT"],"Factor VIII sits in the intrinsic pathway, which aPTT measures; the extrinsic pathway and platelets are unaffected. A prolonged bleeding time suggests a platelet or vWF problem."],
  ["Von Willebrand disease causes bleeding mainly because:","Platelets cannot adhere properly to damaged vessel walls",["Fibrinogen cannot be converted to fibrin","Vitamin K cannot be absorbed","Factor IX is deficient"],"vWF anchors platelets to collagen; without it the platelet plug fails and bleeding is typically immediate and mucosal. It also carries factor VIII, which is why aPTT can be mildly prolonged, but the usual primary defect is adhesion."],
  ["A patient reports easy bruising, frequent nosebleeds and gum bleeding on brushing. These features point most strongly to a defect in:","Primary haemostasis (platelets or von Willebrand factor)",["The extrinsic coagulation pathway","Fibrinolysis","Vitamin K absorption"],"Mucosal and skin-surface bleeding is the signature of a platelet-type problem; factor deficiencies produce delayed and deep bleeding."],
  ["Tranexamic acid mouthwash helps control post-extraction bleeding by:","Inhibiting the conversion of plasminogen to plasmin",["Stimulating platelet aggregation","Replacing factor VIII","Constricting the socket vessels"],"Tranexamic acid is an antifibrinolytic: it preserves the fibrin clot against salivary and tissue plasmin. It does not add factors or platelets."],
  ["A patient taking apixaban asks whether an INR is needed before an extraction. The correct explanation is that:","Apixaban is a direct factor Xa inhibitor and its effect is not measured by the INR",["Apixaban is a vitamin K antagonist and the INR must be at or below 3.5","Apixaban only affects platelets, so a platelet count is needed","Apixaban is reversed by vitamin K before surgery"],"Direct oral anticoagulants act on Xa or thrombin directly; INR is a warfarin tool. Vitamin K does not reverse them."],
  ["Which of the following best explains why oral wounds tend to re-bleed more than skin wounds?","Saliva contains fibrinolytic activity that breaks down the clot",["Oral mucosa has no platelets","The oral cavity is sterile","Oral vessels lack smooth muscle"],"Salivary fibrinolysis and constant movement destabilise the clot, which is why antifibrinolytic mouthwash is useful. The other options are false."],
  ["A dentist prescribes a course of metronidazole to a patient stabilised on warfarin. The most likely consequence is:","A rise in INR and increased bleeding risk",["A fall in INR and increased clotting risk","No change, because antibiotics do not interact with warfarin","Immediate platelet dysfunction"],"Metronidazole inhibits warfarin metabolism, raising its effect. A fall in INR is the wrong direction for this drug; most antibiotics used in dentistry raise it or leave it unchanged."],
  ["Immediately after an extraction the socket oozes continuously and will not form a stable plug, but the patient has no family history and takes no medications. The platelet count is very low. Which mechanism explains the bleeding?","Failure of the platelet plug (primary haemostasis)",["Failure of fibrin formation (secondary haemostasis)","Excess fibrinolysis","Vitamin K deficiency"],"Immediate oozing with a low platelet count is a primary haemostasis failure. Fibrin failure would permit an initial plug and bleed later."],
  ["Which factor is the central enzyme that converts fibrinogen to fibrin?","Thrombin (factor IIa)",["Factor VIII","Tissue factor","Plasmin"],"Both pathways converge on factor X, which generates thrombin; thrombin cleaves fibrinogen into fibrin. Plasmin does the opposite."]
 ]
