 questions: [
  ["The final saliva that enters the mouth is hypotonic relative to plasma because:","Striated duct cells reabsorb sodium and chloride but are nearly impermeable to water",["Acinar cells secrete a hypotonic fluid","Ducts actively secrete water into the lumen","Mucins bind sodium in the lumen"],"The acinar primary fluid is isotonic; the ducts remove salt without water, leaving a dilute saliva. Mucins do not control tonicity."],
  ["Compared with resting saliva, saliva produced at a high flow rate has:","Higher sodium and bicarbonate concentrations",["Lower sodium and bicarbonate concentrations","Higher potassium and lower bicarbonate","The same composition at every flow rate"],"At high flow there is less time for ductal sodium reabsorption and bicarbonate secretion rises, so stimulated saliva approaches plasma and buffers better. The reverse pattern describes resting saliva."],
  ["Which gland produces a purely serous secretion?","Parotid",["Submandibular","Sublingual","Minor palatal glands"],"The parotid is entirely serous; the submandibular is mixed, and the sublingual and most minor glands are mainly mucous."],
  ["The parasympathetic secretomotor fibres to the submandibular and sublingual glands travel with the:","Chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve",["Auriculotemporal branch of the mandibular nerve","Lesser petrosal nerve","Lingual nerve from its origin"],"Facial nerve fibres leave in the chorda tympani, join the lingual nerve and synapse in the submandibular ganglion. The lesser petrosal and auriculotemporal nerves serve the parotid."],
  ["A patient starts a tricyclic antidepressant and within weeks complains of dry mouth. The mechanism is:","Blockade of muscarinic receptors reduces parasympathetic secretion",["Stimulation of sympathetic receptors increases mucin","Direct damage to acinar cells","Blockade of bicarbonate secretion in the ducts"],"Tricyclics have anticholinergic activity, removing the parasympathetic drive that produces watery flow. Acinar damage is the mechanism of radiotherapy, not of drugs."],
  ["Which salivary component is chiefly responsible for buffering plaque acid after a sugar exposure?","Bicarbonate",["Amylase","Lysozyme","Mucin"],"Bicarbonate is the dominant buffer, especially in stimulated saliva. Amylase digests starch and lysozyme is antibacterial; neither buffers."],
  ["Chewing sugar-free gum after meals reduces caries risk primarily because:","It stimulates saliva flow, increasing buffering and clearance",["Gum base mechanically removes plaque","Sweeteners kill cariogenic bacteria","It raises salivary amylase"],"Stimulated saliva is bicarbonate-rich and rapidly clears acid and sugar from plaque. The mechanical and antibacterial effects are minor."],
  ["A 55-year-old man six months after radiotherapy for an oropharyngeal carcinoma develops rapidly progressing caries on the cervical and incisal surfaces of many teeth. The most likely explanation is:","Lasting hyposalivation from radiation damage to serous acini",["Increased sugar intake during treatment","Radiation weakening of enamel crystals directly","Reactivation of a viral infection"],"Serous acinar cells are highly radiosensitive and the loss is often permanent, removing buffering, clearance and remineralisation. Direct radiation damage to enamel is minor compared with loss of saliva."],
  ["A 48-year-old woman reports dry mouth and gritty, dry eyes, and has bilateral parotid enlargement. The most likely diagnosis is:","Sjögren's syndrome",["Drug-induced xerostomia","Mumps","Sialolithiasis"],"Dry mouth with dry eyes and glandular enlargement is the classic autoimmune picture. Mumps is acute and painful, and a stone usually affects one submandibular gland with mealtime swelling."],
  ["The acquired enamel pellicle is best described as:","A film of adsorbed salivary proteins on the tooth surface that forms within minutes of cleaning",["A layer of bacteria and their products","Remnants of the enamel organ left after eruption","A mineral deposit from supersaturated saliva"],"Pellicle is acellular and protein-based; plaque forms on top of it as bacteria attach. Enamel organ remnants form the primary cuticle, which is lost soon after eruption."],
  ["Which protein keeps saliva supersaturated with calcium and phosphate without spontaneous precipitation?","Statherin",["Amylase","Lactoferrin","Secretory IgA"],"Statherin and proline-rich proteins inhibit crystal growth, keeping the mineral available for remineralisation. Lactoferrin binds iron to starve bacteria."],
  ["Sympathetic stimulation of a salivary gland produces:","A small volume of viscous, protein-rich saliva",["A large volume of watery saliva","Complete cessation of all secretion","A large volume of bicarbonate-rich saliva"],"Sympathetic input drives protein exocytosis with little fluid, which is why anxiety gives a thick, sticky mouth. Copious watery flow is parasympathetic."],
  ["Which statement best describes the effect of healthy ageing on salivary flow?","A modest fall; marked hyposalivation points to drugs or disease",["Complete loss of flow by the age of 80","A rise in flow from gland hypertrophy","A fall of more than half in every person"],"Whole-saliva flow is modestly lower in healthy older adults, even without medication, but marked hyposalivation should prompt a search for drugs or disease. It does not cease, and the glands do not hypertrophy with age."],
  ["Which salivary antimicrobial protein has particular activity against Candida?","Histatins",["Amylase","Statherin","Mucin"],"Histatins are antifungal peptides; their loss in hyposalivation contributes to oral candidiasis. Amylase and statherin are not antifungal agents."],
  ["Loss of mucous saliva affects complete denture wearers mainly because:","Mucins provide the lubricating film needed for comfort and retention",["Mucins harden the acrylic base","Mucous saliva dissolves plaque on the denture","Mucins supply fluoride to the ridge"],"The thin mucin film between denture and mucosa gives adhesion, cohesion and lubrication; without it dentures rub and lose retention. Fluoride delivery is not a mucin function."]
 ]
